        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Anker SOLIX Lineup Explained: C Series vs F Series
          </h2>

          <p>
            Anker sells its power stations under the SOLIX brand, and the lineup splits into two families. The C series is the carry-it-yourself range: everything from the 288Wh C300X up to the 2,048Wh C2000 Gen 2. The F series is the roll-it-around range, starting at the 2,048Wh F2000 and topping out with the 3,840Wh F3800 Plus. The letter tells you more than the number. C units are designed to be lifted into a car; F units have wheels and telescoping handles because they weigh 65 to 137 lb.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Model Numbers Mean</h3>

          <p>
            The number after the letter is a rough guide to capacity in watt-hours, not output. A C1000 holds about 1,000Wh, a C2000 about 2,000Wh, and an F3800 about 3,800Wh. That is a welcome change from the older naming, but it is not exact: the C1000 holds 1,056Wh, the C1000 Gen 2 holds 1,024Wh, and the C800 Plus holds 768Wh. Read the spec sheet before assuming the name equals the capacity.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What the Suffixes Mean</h3>

          <p>
            Suffixes add another layer. An X usually means the same hardware sold through a particular retail channel or in a different finish, as with the C1000X. Plus usually means a mid-cycle refresh with better charging or extra features, as with the C800 Plus and its built-in light or the F3800 Plus and its higher solar input. Gen 2 means a genuinely new design, and in Anker's case that has meant faster charging, more output and lower weight. DC means the inverter has been removed, so there are no wall-style outlets at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose by Job, Not by Name</h3>

          <p>
            The practical way to choose is by job, not by name. For camping, CPAP and charging electronics, a C-series unit between 768Wh and 1,024Wh is right for most people. For a fridge, freezer and furnace through an overnight outage, the C2000 Gen 2 or the F3000 is the realistic floor. For 240V loads, whole-home circuits or multi-day outages, only the F3800 Plus will do. If you are still working out how much battery you need, our guide to <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size portable power station you need</a> walks through the math appliance by appliance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solarbank and the E10 Are Different Products</h3>

          <p>
            Anker also makes balcony and rooftop solar storage systems under the SOLIX name, including the Solarbank line and the E10 whole-home system. Those are installed products rather than portable power stations, so they are outside this ranking. If someone tells you to buy an Anker battery for your house, make sure you know which of these three very different categories they mean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="powerhouse" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Anker PowerHouse vs SOLIX: The Old Names, and the Anker Power Plant Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before 2023, Anker sold its power stations as PowerHouse models with three-digit numbers: the PowerHouse 521, 535, 555, 757 and 767, among others. When the company launched SOLIX as its energy brand, many of those products were renamed rather than replaced. The PowerHouse 767 became the SOLIX F2000, and the PowerHouse 757 was sold for a time as the SOLIX F1200. The hardware did not change on day one; the box did.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shopping Used or on Clearance</h3>

          <p>
            That matters when you shop used or on clearance. A listing for a PowerHouse 767 and a listing for a SOLIX F2000 can be the same 2,048Wh, LiFePO4, roughly 67 lb machine, and you should compare them on price, condition and remaining warranty rather than treating the SOLIX version as newer technology. The F2000 is still a capable battery, but its charging and solar input trail the C2000 Gen 2, which holds the same 2,048Wh in a far lighter box. Unless the F2000 is heavily discounted, the C2000 Gen 2 is the better buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NMC vs LiFePO4 Cells</h3>

          <p>
            Some older PowerHouse models used NMC lithium-ion cells rather than LiFePO4. The current SOLIX lineup has standardized on LiFePO4, which trades a little weight for far longer cycle life and better thermal stability. If you are considering a cheap older PowerHouse, check the chemistry on the label. Our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion power stations</a> covers why that difference is worth paying for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Anker Power Plant Question</h3>

          <p>
            You will also see people search for an Anker power plant. Anker does not sell a product by that name in the US. The phrase is shorthand people use for a big Anker battery that can run a house, and in practice it points at the F3800 Plus or an F3000 pair. Nobody needs a power plant; you need enough watt-hours to cover your outage and enough watts to start your largest motor, and the sections below show how to size both.
          </p>
        </div>

